{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "tasks": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", ".next/**", "!.next/cache/**"]
    },
    "dev": {
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["coverage/**"],
      // `cache: false` is a CORRECTNESS setting, not a preference, and
      // `apps/api/src/__isolation__/integration-coverage.test.ts` asserts it.
      //
      // Turbo's cache is content-addressed: it assumes a task's result is a function of
      // its inputs. These suites are not — they talk to Postgres (:55432), ClickHouse
      // (:58123) and Docker, and their pass/fail depends on state no hash covers.
      // Measured: three consecutive runs of an unchanged package gave `cache hit,
      // replaying logs`, so a package whose files had not changed would replay a green
      // STRICT result after ClickHouse or Docker had gone down, and the named failure
      // from integration-coverage.test.ts would never run — this branch's own defect,
      // laundered through the cache. This repository has also already had turbo replay
      // ANOTHER worktree's test results as green. `--force` hides that rather than
      // fixing it, which is why caching is off here instead of documented as a flag
      // people must remember.
      "cache": false,
      // Still required with the cache off: turbo 2 runs strict env mode, so an
      // UNDECLARED variable never reaches the task at all and
      // DECKGAUGE_TEST_STRICT_INTEGRATION=1 on the root script would be a silent no-op.
      "env": ["DECKGAUGE_TEST_STRICT_INTEGRATION"]
    },
    // EVERY workspace has this task. It began as `apps/web`-only, because that
    // app was typechecked by nothing — `typescript.ignoreBuildErrors` was set, no
    // script ran `tsc`, and it carried 85 type errors while lint/build/test were
    // green. `next build` is NOT a substitute there, and this was verified by
    // probe rather than assumed: with `ignoreBuildErrors` removed it fails on a
    // type error in a SOURCE file (good) but PASSES with one in a `.test.tsx` —
    // Next does not check test files at all, and most of the 85 were in test
    // files. `tsc --noEmit` over the app's own tsconfig is what sees them.
    //
    // The same hole existed in every other package by three different
    // mechanisms (a tsconfig `exclude`, a `tsup` build that walks only
    // `src/index.ts`, and an `include` that never reached `packages/db/__tests__/`),
    // hiding 1,220 errors. Each package now runs `tsc -p tsconfig.typecheck.json`
    // — its own tsconfig minus that `exclude`, emitting nothing. See CLAUDE.md
    // § `pnpm typecheck`.
    //
    // `dependsOn: ["build"]` is each package's OWN build, not `^build`, and the
    // reason differs by package — both reasons are real, so do not "simplify"
    // this to one of them:
    //   - `apps/web`: its tsconfig includes `.next/types/**/*.ts`, which
    //     `next build` generates. Typecheck against a stale `.next` and you get
    //     TS2307 for a page that no longer exists (observed after deleting a route).
    //   - everywhere else: the probe sets `composite: false`, so it does NOT
    //     follow project references — it resolves `@deckgauge/db` and
    //     `@deckgauge/shared` through their built `.d.ts` in `dist/`. Turbo's
    //     `build` task is itself `dependsOn: ["^build"]`, so depending on this
    //     package's build transitively guarantees those exist.
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["build"]
    },
    "lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^lint"]
    }
  }
}
